# Scaladoc for classes

When writing or completing Scaladoc on classes (including `case class`), follow this format.

## Required structure

1. **Description** — at most **10 lines** summarizing the overall meaning and responsibility of the class (not implementation detail). No empty `TODO` stubs. This limit applies only to the descriptive prose, **not** to the full Scaladoc block (tags may make the comment longer).
2. **`@param name description`** — one tag per class attribute exposed by the primary constructor (e.g. `val` / `var` parameters and case-class fields). Name must match the attribute; include a short description after the name. Omit `@param` only when the class has no such attributes.
3. **Construction guidance** — required when the **primary constructor is `private`** (or otherwise not meant for callers) **and** alternative construction methods exist (companion `apply` / factory methods, or a dedicated factory class). Tell the reader to use those alternatives and link to them with Scaladoc wiki links (`[[...]]`).
4. **`@since X.Y.Z`** — required on every **newly created** class. Set `X.Y.Z` to the current `ThisBuild / version` value in the project-root `build.sbt` (e.g. `"0.1.0"` → `@since 0.1.0`). Do not invent a version; read it from `build.sbt`.

Block / tag order: description → construction guidance (if applicable) → `@param` → `@since`.

## Construction alternatives

When documenting private construction, name the intended entry points and link them:

- Companion methods: `[[MyClass.apply]]`, `[[MyClass.fromUri]]`, etc.
- Factory type / methods: `[[MyClassFactory]]`, `[[MyClassFactory.create]]`, etc.

Prefer one short sentence plus links over listing every overload.

## Do / don't

```scala
// ❌ BAD — empty TODO, no @param for attributes, no @since, no companion pointer
/**
 * TODO
 */
class Token private (val value: String)

object Token {
  def apply(raw: String): Token = new Token(raw.trim)
}

// ✅ GOOD — short description (≤10 lines), @param per attribute, construction link, @since
/**
 * Immutable authentication token used by HTTP requests.
 *
 * Do not instantiate directly; use [[Token.apply]] instead.
 *
 * @param value raw token string
 * @since 0.1.0
 */
class Token private (val value: String)

object Token {
  def apply(raw: String): Token = new Token(raw.trim)
}
```

```scala
// ✅ GOOD — dedicated factory class
/**
 * Parsed ArcGIS service endpoint.
 *
 * Construction is private; create instances via [[ServiceEndpointFactory.create]].
 *
 * @param uri absolute service URI
 * @since 0.1.0
 */
class ServiceEndpoint private (val uri: String)
```

```scala
// ✅ GOOD — public primary constructor; @param for each field; no construction guidance
/**
 * Configuration for STTP multipart form encoding.
 *
 * @param boundary multipart boundary string
 * @since 0.1.0
 */
case class MultipartSettings(boundary: String)
```

```scala
// ✅ GOOD — multiple attributes each get @param; description stays short
/**
 * Credentials used to request an ArcGIS generate-token response.
 *
 * @param username account user name
 * @param password account password
 * @param referer  optional HTTP referer sent with the request
 * @since 0.1.0
 */
case class GenerateTokenCredentials(
  username: String,
  password: String,
  referer: Option[String]
)
```

## Notes

- The **description** must be ≤ 10 lines; `@param`, construction guidance, and `@since` do not count toward that limit.
- Always fill in text after each `@param` — bare tags are invalid.
- Do not document every method on the class Scaladoc; members have their own docs (see the methods Scaladoc rule).
- `@since` applies to **new** classes only; do not rewrite `@since` on existing classes unless the user asks.
- When completing incomplete Scaladoc (e.g. `TODO`), apply this full template.
- This rule targets **classes**; method Scaladoc is covered by `scaladoc-methods`.
